---
id: org.dxos.review.process
name: ProcessRules
version: 0.1.0
---

Repo-process and build-config conventions mined from a year of human review
comments on `dxos/dxos`, clustered into candidate rules in
`.agents/projects/architecture-rules/dataset/CANDIDATES.md`. Each `rule` block
below is discovered by the agentic-review harness, matched against the files
its globs select, and applied by a focused subagent.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                   |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| `rule` | org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0 |

## Rules

```mdl
rule catalog-is-dependency-source-of-truth: Bump, add, and remove dependency versions in the workspace catalog, not per-package.json
  `pnpm-workspace.yaml`'s catalog is the one source of truth for a
  dependency's version; bumping it directly in a package's own `package.json`
  instead creates a second place that can disagree with the catalog, and a
  catalog entry left behind after its last consumer is removed is a version
  nobody is pinning to anymore. Flag a version bump made directly in a
  `package.json` `dependencies`/`devDependencies` entry instead of the
  catalog; a catalog entry with no remaining consumer in the change set; a
  build- or dev-only tool listed under `dependencies` instead of
  `devDependencies`. Do not flag a `package.json` entry correctly pointing at
  the catalog (`catalog:`/`workspace:*`) left unchanged. Fix: move the bump
  into `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, delete the orphaned entry, reclassify the dep.
  Source: 3 review comments across 3 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10380#discussion_r2674394262.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/package.json
    - pnpm-workspace.yaml
  unit: pr
  context: diff, package
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule ci-and-tooling-avoid-duplicate-mechanisms: Don't stand up a second CI step or shared setting that duplicates one that already covers the concern
  This specializes `one-mechanism-per-concern` with process-specific
  boundaries a repo-wide architecture rule can't derive without knowing what
  the existing CI graph and settings already cover: a full-repo lint step
  added beside an existing affected-only lint step, or a personal editor
  preference committed into shared project settings so it silently applies to
  every contributor. Flag a new CI workflow step whose coverage an existing
  step already provides; an editor/IDE preference added to a checked-in
  shared settings file that is really a personal choice. Do not flag a
  genuinely new CI check with no existing overlap, or a project-wide setting
  the whole team needs, correctly placed in shared config. Fix: drop the
  duplicate step, or move the preference to user-level settings.
  Source: 3 review comments across 3 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10685#discussion_r2891618104.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - .github/workflows/**/*.yml
    - .vscode/**/*.json
  unit: pr
  context: diff, pr
  system-one: off
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule diff-scoped-to-pr-purpose: Keep a diff scoped to what the PR says it does; drop unrelated or accidental hunks
  A hunk that reformats, reorders, or otherwise edits code the PR's stated
  purpose gives no reason to touch is usually leftover from a bad merge or an
  editor auto-format, and it costs a reviewer time disentangling it from the
  actual change — or worse, it lands unreviewed inside a change nobody meant
  to make. Flag a hunk that reformats or reorders code with no connection to
  what the PR description says the change does. Do not flag an incidental
  change the PR description explicitly calls out and justifies (a named
  drive-by fix). Fix: revert the unrelated hunk so the diff matches the PR
  description.
  Source: 2 review comments across 2 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10527#discussion_r2863781713.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
    - packages/**/*.md
  unit: pr
  context: diff, pr
  system-one: on
  question: Does this diff contain a hunk with no stated connection to the PR's description?
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule moon-yml-entrypoint-registration: Every package.json export/import entrypoint must be registered in the package's moon.yml
  Adding an entrypoint to `package.json`'s `exports`/`imports` without also
  registering the matching path in the same package's `moon.yml` compile
  configuration means the build silently misses it — the entrypoint resolves
  in dev but is absent from the published build with no error anywhere. Flag
  a `package.json` `exports`/`imports` entry that is added or changed with no
  matching update to the same package's `moon.yml`. Do not flag an entrypoint
  that already maps to a registered glob/pattern in `moon.yml`. Fix: add the
  new path to `moon.yml`'s compile configuration.
  Source: 5 review comments across 1 PR, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/10452#discussion_r2725564421.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/package.json
  unit: file
  context: diff, siblings
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

## Appendix

```mdl
ext rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0
  desc: A machine-checked review rule applied by an LLM subagent or the System One checker.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    files:       Glob | Glob[]     # paths this rule reviews, relative to `scope`
    grep?:       Prose             # optional regex pre-filter over file contents
    severity?:   warn | error      # defaults to warn
    scope?:      dir | repo        # glob base: this file's dir (default) or repo root
    unit?:       file | pr         # one verdict per file (default) or per change set
    context?:    ContextKind[]     # diff | imports | importers | siblings | package | public-api | similar | test | pr
    system-one?: on | off          # off when no fetcher can supply what the rule needs
    question?:   Prose             # literal yes/no question for the System One checker
```
